{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CggJfnS262rzEgyaByccM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No tools are available to me in this session — no BLAST, no database lookup, no shell. So the identification below comes from my own knowledge of the sequence, and I flag my confidence accordingly. I counted the residues by hand from the sequence you pasted: **330 aa**.\n\n## What it is\n\nThis is **asparagine synthetase A (AsnA)** from *Escherichia coli* — gene `asnA`, UniProt **P00963** (ASNA_ECOLI), 330 residues, ~36.7 kDa, cytoplasmic, no signal peptide.\n\nWhat makes me confident:\n\n- The N-terminus `MKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHFSRQLEERLGLIEVQAPILSR…` is the distinctive start of P00963.\n- The length (330) matches the canonical P00963 sequence exactly.\n- Near the C-terminus you have `…PQTIGGGIGQSRLTMLLL…` — the `GGGIGQSR` block is **motif 3 of the class II aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase fold**, which AsnA carries because it is an evolutionary offshoot of aspartyl-tRNA synthetase (see below).\n\n## What it does\n\n**EC 6.3.1.1, aspartate—ammonia ligase.** It makes L-asparagine using free ammonia:\n\n```\nL-aspartate + NH4+ + ATP  →  L-asparagine + AMP + PPi\n```\n\nThe mechanism is two-step, through a tightly bound **β-aspartyl-AMP** intermediate: ATP attacks the aspartate β-carboxylate to release pyrophosphate, then ammonia displaces AMP. Note it is an **AMP + PPi**-forming ligase, not ADP + Pi — that is the class II aaRS chemistry showing through.\n\nOther points worth knowing:\n\n- **Quaternary structure:** homodimer. Crystal structure of *E. coli* AsnA solved in the late 1990s (PDB **12AS**, Nakatsu et al.); the fold is an antiparallel β-sheet catalytic domain essentially superimposable on the AspRS catalytic domain, minus the tRNA-binding domain. AsnA is generally read as having arisen from AspRS by loss of that domain, with the activated β-aspartyl-adenylate retained and the tRNA acceptor replaced by ammonia.\n- **Not the only route in *E. coli*.** The other enzyme, **AsnB** (asparagine synthetase B, EC 6.3.5.4, ~554 aa), is glutamine-dependent and has an N-terminal glutaminase domain. Either one alone supports growth; an `asnA asnB` double mutant is an asparagine auxotroph. Because AsnA needs high free NH4+, it matters most in ammonia-rich conditions.\n- **Regulation:** `asnA` transcription is controlled by **AsnC**, an Lrp-family regulator, with asparagine acting as the effector.\n- **No human orthologue of this type.** Humans have only the glutamine-dependent ASNS. That asymmetry is the basis of L-asparaginase therapy in acute lymphoblastic leukaemia, and bacterial AsnA/AsnB are the reason asparagine auxotrophy engineering is done in bacteria.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- I could not run an alignment, so I cannot certify the sequence residue-by-residue against P00963. If this is an engineered point mutant or a non-K-12 strain variant, a single substitution would be invisible to me. If that distinction matters, run `blastp` against UniProt/`nr` and check for 100% identity versus one or two mismatches.\n- Confidence: **high** on the identification as *E. coli* AsnA / asparagine synthetase A (distinctive N-terminus + exact length + class II aaRS motif 3). **Not verified** for exact residue-level identity. No E-value to report, since no search was run.\n\n## ANSWER\n\n*E. coli* **asparagine synthetase A (AsnA)**, UniProt **P00963**, 330 aa, EC **6.3.1.1** (aspartate—ammonia ligase).\nIt makes L-asparagine from L-aspartate + ammonia + ATP via a β-aspartyl-AMP intermediate, releasing AMP + pyrophosphate; a homodimer with a class II aspartyl-tRNA-synthetase-like catalytic fold (PDB 12AS).\nIdentified from sequence knowledge, not a database search — confirm with `blastp` if exact strain/variant identity matters."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19224,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_